    "claim": "The ndata crate intentionally subverts many of rust's strengths in order to overcome its weaknesses. Don't use a DataArray when a Vec will do, but you should understand the advantages of a DataArray and when using it makes sense -- code boundaries, function signatures, closures, threads. Same goes for HashMap vs DataObject and byte arrays vs DataBytes.",
    "tags": "doctrine,ndata,rust",
    "source": {
      "doc": "the owner, in session, 2026-08-05"
    },
    "confidence": "high",
    "time": 1785935667274
  },
  {
    "claim": "Flowlang provides the functional substrate for all of newbound. Newbound provides a UI, cohesion, data integrity, reusable workflows, a security framework and a pervasive peer-to-peer paradigm.",
    "tags": "doctrine,architecture,flowlang",
    "source": {
      "doc": "the owner, in session, 2026-08-05"
    },
    "confidence": "high",
    "time": 1785935667280
  },
  {
    "claim": "The Newbound design philosophy is FACET-ORIENTED -- neither OO nor FP. Every thing is a record, and data and functionality are facets of that one identity; neither encapsulates the other. OO hides state behind methods, but Newbound's state is open and addressable, with access governed by readers/writers rather than encapsulation. FP wants immutability, but ndata restores shared mutable object graphs on purpose -- the Java object model Newbound was ported away from, deliberately reconstructed inside Rust because that part of Java was a strength.",
    "tags": "doctrine,philosophy,facet-oriented,architecture",
    "source": {
      "doc": "articulated in session and blessed by the owner verbatim, 2026-08-05"
    },
    "confidence": "high",
    "time": 1785936720274
  },
  {
    "claim": "Newbound is one live, journaled, secured, distributed object graph in which code is data -- a command, a flow, a scene, an image, a memory are all records with attachments in one store -- and the pervasive concerns (security, history, peers) are woven by the substrate rather than written by the user.",
    "tags": "doctrine,philosophy,architecture",
    "source": {
      "doc": "articulated in session and blessed by the owner verbatim, 2026-08-05"
    },
    "confidence": "high",
    "time": 1785936720281
  },
  {
    "claim": "The scratch library is required by the agent library. Its purpose is to provide a safe hot-swappable scratchpad for the agent to build both transient code as well as 'skills' in the traditional agentic-harness sense. No production code lives there.",
